  {
    "episode_id": 86550,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86550.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "That's also just one point of the changes in the child tax credit in this bill. There's three other items that we're doing. One, we're eliminating what I refer to as the child penalty. You know, there's a work requirement for the child tax credit where you have to work so much before you can qualify and how current law is. It allows it where work requirement for your first child and your second child and your third child. But once you earn it with your first child under this bill, You earn it, whether you have two children, whether you have three children or four children. That is a huge provision that will help a lot of working families. The other thing that we're doing is the top line $2,000. It's been $2,000 since 2017. It has not changed, even though inflation's gone up 20%. But we're starting to index it for inflation. starting moving forward of the 2000s. So the top line child tax credit will continue to grow as well. So there's a couple of different levers. And the issue is, is that, you know, when you're in divided government, where my counterparts on the Senate side are controlled by Democrats, we're controlled by Republicans, you have to find some common ground. And this is where we were able to get common ground when you're looking at an $80 billion tax bill.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The bill includes more than one change to the child tax credit.",
        "confidence": 0.98
      },
      {
        "text": "The current child tax credit cap is $2,000 per child, unchanged since 2017.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "One of the changes eliminates the 'child penalty' associated with work requirements for the child tax credit.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Under current law, eligibility for the child tax credit depends on earning income for each additional child beyond the first.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Under the proposed bill, once a family earns eligibility for the first child, they retain it for all subsequent children regardless of additional earnings.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Inflation has increased by approximately 20% since 2017.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The bill introduces indexing of the child tax credit to inflation going forward.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Indexing ensures the maximum child tax credit grows over time with inflation.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "This legislation is part of an $80 billion tax bill.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The bill was developed through bipartisan negotiation due to divided government between the House and Senate.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
